from lessons import L
L(9, 'Loops: for, range, while', '🔁', 30,
  'Repeat steps with for and while loops, and predict exactly how many times they run.',
  ['I can write for i in range(...) and say which numbers it makes',
   'I can add up or count things with an accumulator',
   'I can write a while loop and make sure it ends',
   'I can use break, continue, and read N numbers in a loop'],
  [
  ('p', 'Say you want to print <code>Hello!</code> five times. You could copy and paste the line five times. But what about 500 times? Computers are great at repeating, so we use a <b>loop</b>: write the steps once, and Python repeats them.'),
  ('try', 'for i in range(5):\n    print("Hello!")', [], 'Predict how many lines you will see. Run it. Then change the 5 to 3 and run again.'),
  ('rule', 'The shape of a for loop',
   '<code>for i in range(5):</code> &nbsp;ends with a <b>colon</b>. The lines <b>indented</b> underneath (the <b>body</b>) run once for every round. The variable <code>i</code> gets a new value each round.'),
  ('trace', 'for i in range(3):\n    print("round", i)', []),
  ('trap', '<b>range(5) is 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.</b> It <b>starts at 0</b> and <b>stops BEFORE 5</b>. So range(5) makes 5 rounds, but the last value is 4, not 5.'),
  ('h', 'range(start, stop, step)'),
  ('table', ['Code', 'The numbers it makes', 'Why'], [
     ['<code>range(5)</code>', '0 1 2 3 4', 'starts at 0, stops before 5'],
     ['<code>range(1, 5)</code>', '1 2 3 4', 'starts AT 1, stops before 5'],
     ['<code>range(2, 10, 2)</code>', '2 4 6 8', 'start 2, step +2, stops before 10'],
     ['<code>range(5, 0, -1)</code>', '5 4 3 2 1', 'a negative step counts DOWN; 0 is not reached']]),
  ('try', 'for i in range(5, 0, -1):\n    print(i)\nprint("Liftoff!")', [], 'A countdown! Notice that <code>print("Liftoff!")</code> is not indented, so it runs once, after the loop. Try range(10, 0, -2).'),
  ('quick', 'Which numbers does range(1, 4) make?', ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 3, 4', '0, 1, 2, 3', '2, 3, 4'], 0, 'It starts AT 1 and stops BEFORE 4, so you get 1, 2, 3.'),
  ('h', 'The accumulator: adding things up'),
  ('p', 'Loops are great for totals. Make a variable that starts at <b>0</b> before the loop, then add to it inside the loop. This is called an <b>accumulator</b>. Watch the table: <code>total</code> grows each round.'),
  ('trace', 'total = 0\nfor i in range(1, 4):\n    total = total + i\nprint(total)', []),
  ('rule', 'Accumulator recipe',
   '1) Create it <b>before</b> the loop: <code>total = 0</code> for a sum or a count, <code>result = 1</code> for a product (multiplying).<br>2) Update it <b>inside</b> the loop: <code>total = total + i</code>.<br>3) Print it <b>after</b> the loop (not indented).'),
  ('try', 'result = 1\nfor i in range(1, 6):\n    result = result * i\nprint(result)', [], 'This multiplies 1 x 2 x 3 x 4 x 5. Change the starting <code>result = 1</code> to 0 and see why a product must start at 1.'),
  ('trap', '<b>Indentation decides what repeats.</b> If <code>print(total)</code> is indented, it is part of the loop and prints on every round. If it is not indented, it prints once at the end. Also: <code>total = 0</code> inside the loop would reset the total to 0 every round.'),
  ('show', 'total = 0\nfor i in range(1, 4):\n    total = total + i\n    print(total)', []),
  ('h', 'while: repeat as long as something is True'),
  ('p', 'Remember <code>if</code>: it checks a condition <b>once</b> and runs its lines <b>once</b>. A <code>while</code> loop checks the condition <b>before every round</b> and keeps going while it is True. Use <code>for</code> when you know how many rounds; use <code>while</code> when you do not.'),
  ('trace', 'n = 1\nwhile n <= 3:\n    print(n)\n    n = n + 1\nprint("done")', []),
  ('rule', 'A while loop needs three things',
   '1) a <b>starting value</b> (<code>n = 1</code>), 2) a <b>condition</b> (<code>n &lt;= 3</code>), 3) something <b>inside the loop that changes</b> the value (<code>n = n + 1</code>).'),
  ('trap', '<b>If nothing in the loop changes the condition, the loop never ends.</b> If you forgot <code>n = n + 1</code>, n would stay 1 and <code>n &lt;= 3</code> would be True forever. Also watch <code>&lt;</code> vs <code>&lt;=</code>: <code>while n &lt; 3</code> stops one round earlier than <code>while n &lt;= 3</code>.'),
  ('h', 'break and continue'),
  ('try', 'for i in range(1, 8):\n    if i == 3:\n        continue\n    if i == 6:\n        break\n    print(i)', [], 'Predict the output first! <b>continue</b> skips the rest of THIS round. <b>break</b> leaves the whole loop. Then change 6 to 5 and run again.'),
  ('h', 'Loops with input, and printing on one line'),
  ('p', 'To read N numbers: first read N, then loop N times. When you do not need the loop variable, the name <code>_</code> is a common choice. To keep printing on the same line, add <code>end=""</code> (or <code>end=" "</code> for a space).'),
  ('try', 'n = int(input())\ntotal = 0\nfor _ in range(n):\n    total = total + int(input())\nprint("Sum:", total)', ['3', '10', '20', '30'], 'The first number (3) says how many come next. Try typing 2, 5, 8 instead.'),
  ('show', 'for i in range(1, 4):\n    for j in range(1, 4):\n        print(i * j, end=" ")\n    print()', []),
  ('p', 'That is a <b>nested loop</b> (a loop inside a loop): for each row i, the inner loop runs completely, and the bare <code>print()</code> ends the row. It makes a small multiplication table.'),
  ('h', 'Common loop mistakes'),
  ('table', ['Mistake', 'What happens'], [
     ['<code>for i in range(5)</code> (no colon)', 'SyntaxError'],
     ['Loop body not indented', 'IndentationError'],
     ['<code>range(n)</code> where n came from <code>input()</code> (still text)', 'TypeError - use int(input())'],
     ['<code>range(2.5)</code> or a float from float(input())', 'TypeError - range needs a whole number'],
     ['Using <code>total</code> in the loop without <code>total = 0</code> before it', 'NameError'],
     ['<code>range(1, 5)</code> when you wanted 1 to 5', 'No error, but 5 is missing - use range(1, 6)'],
     ['A while loop that never changes its counter', 'No error message - it simply never ends']]),
  ('quick', 'What does this print?  total = 0, then for i in range(4): total = total + i, then print(total)', ['6', '10', '4', '3'], 0, 'i is 0, 1, 2, 3 and 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.'),
  ('quick', 'You want to add up the numbers 1 to 10. Which header is right?', ['for i in range(1, 11):', 'for i in range(1, 10):', 'for i in range(10):', 'for i in range(0, 10, 2):'], 0, 'range(1, 11) starts at 1 and stops before 11, so it makes 1 to 10.'),
  ('quick', 'n = 10, then while n > 7: n = n - 1. What is n when the loop stops?', ['7', '8', '6', '10'], 0, 'n goes 10, 9, 8, 7. When n is 7 the test 7 > 7 is False, so the loop stops with n = 7.'),
  ])
